[29] [done] A Garmin `.fit` -> GPX converter, `fit_to_gpx.py`, mirroring `wahoo_to_gpx.py`: read each `.fit` in `fit/` (gitignored, like `gc/`/`gpx/`) with `fitparse`, gather the `record` stream into one GPX 1.1 track in `gpx/` so `trackview.py` renders the Garmin rides alongside the Wahoo/komoot ones.  Same flags (`--ext`, `--since`/`--until`, a positional file, `--out-dir`), the same `--ext` `<trkpt><extensions>` tags, and the same equirectangular `_distance_and_gain`.  The output is named after the ride *start time* the Wahoo way (`YYYY_MM_DD_HH_MM_SS.gpx`, from the `session`/`file_id`/first-record start, never the long Connect device+serial name), so `trackview._ride_date` reads the date off the stem and the Garmin rides sort / filter / sheet like the Wahoo ones; the original `.fit` name stays in the GPX `<desc>`.  The device / sport are reworded to the Wahoo voice (`wahoo_fitness` -> `Wahoo ELEMNT BOLT`, `cycling` -> `Bike`) so a Garmin ride reads the same as a Wahoo one and `trackview --device`/`--sport` match across both; an unknown word passes through.  Fitparse quirks: position is in "semicircles" (and speed m/s, distance m) -> normalised by `StandardUnitsDataProcessor`; the "no altitude" sentinel is `-500 m` (raw 0 against the +500 offset, or an absent field) -> filled like Wahoo's `0 m`; no-fix records dropped; the `--since`/`--until` ride date falls back to the file's `session`/`file_id` start time when the name is a bare serial; `fitparse` added to `requirements.txt`.
     DONE (2026-09-27): `fit_to_gpx.py` + `tests/test_fit_to_gpx.py` (19 tests, a
     hand-built minimal `.fit` since fitparse is a reader).  Verified: a no-fix lead-in
     record is dropped (`src` 4 -> `n` 3), the `-500 m` sentinel is forward/back-filled
     (302 -> 304 -> filled 304), semicircles decode to degrees and 40 m/s -> 144 km/h in
     the `speed_kmh` extension, times carry a UTC "Z", `--ext` embeds all six telemetry
     tags (and a plain ride embeds none), `_ride_date` prefers the filename then falls
     back to the file's start time for a bare-serial name, and `main` converts a folder
     and composes `--since`/`--until`.  Verified also that the output stem is the
     ride start time (Wahoo-style `YYYY_MM_DD_HH_MM_SS`, never the long Garmin
     device+serial name) so a real Connect export still lands on the same stem, and
     that the device / sport reword to the Wahoo voice (`wahoo_fitness` ->
     `Wahoo ELEMNT BOLT`, `cycling` -> `Bike`, unknown words pass through).
     Full suite now 75 pass, 1 skip.

[30] [done] Test the Wahoo ELEMNT JSON -> GPX converter `wahoo_to_gpx.py` the same way `fit_to_gpx.py` is tested ([29]): a hand-built minimal ride JSON (the Wahoo export is plain JSON, so unlike fit it can be synthesised in full, no binary reader needed) driven through `ride_to_gpx` and `main`.  Same shape as the fit test, with the Wahoo quirks: the file is UTF-8 *with a BOM* -> opened `utf-8-sig` so the "RIDE" key still parses; "no fix" rows (`LAT/LON == 0`) at the start *and* end are dropped; the `ALT == 0` "no altitude" sentinel is forward/back-filled (all-zero -> no `<ele>`); absolute time = `STARTTIME` + `SECS` written with a UTC "Z"; `LAT`/`LON` are already decimal degrees and `KPH` already km/h so both pass through untouched; `--ext` embeds the six `power`/`speed_kmh`/`cadence`/`heartrate`/`grade`/`temperature` `<trkpt><extensions>` tags (a plain ride embeds none); the device/sport/athlete land in the `<desc>`/`author` (no rewording, unlike fit); `_ride_date` reads the Wahoo-style stem (no in-file fallback, the stem is always dated); and `main` converts a folder, composes `--since`/`--until`, skips undated and no-fix rides, and names the output after the JSON stem.
